      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the probability that the first card is a 6 and the second card is a Queen when two cards are selected at random from a standard deck of 52 cards. We'll break down the problem into two steps:\n\n1. Calculate the probability of drawing a 6 first.\n2. Calculate the probability of drawing a Queen second, given that a 6 has already been drawn first.\n\n### Step 1: Probability of drawing a 6 first\n\nThere are 52 cards in the deck, and there are 4 of these cards that are 6s. The probability of drawing a 6 first is given by:\n\n\\[ P(\\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{52} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Probability of drawing a Queen second, given a 6 has already been drawn\n\nAfter drawing a 6 first, there are 51 cards left in the deck. There are 4 Queens in the deck. The probability of drawing a Queen second, given that a 6 has already been drawn first, is:\n\n\\[ P(\\text{Second card is a Queen} \\mid \\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{51} \\]\n\n### Combined probability\n\nThe combined probability of both events happening in sequence is the product of the individual probabilities:\n\n\\[ P(\\text{First card is a 6 and second is a Queen}) = \\frac{4}{52} \\times \\frac{4}{51} \\]\n\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\n\\[ P(\\text{First card is a 6 and second is a Queen}) = \\frac{4 \\times 4}{52 \\times 51} = \\frac{16}{2652} = \\frac{4}{663} \\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{4}{663}} \\]\n\nHere is the Python code to confirm this result:\n```python\n# Calculate the probability\r\nprob_first_card_is_6 = 4 / 52\r\nprob_second_card_is_Queen_given_6_first = 4 / 51\r\n\r\n# Combined probability\r\ncombined_probability = prob_first_card_is_6 * prob_second_card_is_Queen_given_6_first\r\n\r\n# Simplify the fraction\r\nfrom fractions import Fraction\r\nfinal_probability = Fraction(combined_probability).limit_denominator()\r\n\r\nprint(final_probability)\n```",
